.TH QFW-DIR-SVC 1 "August 2026" "QFw 0.1.0" "QFw Commands"
.SH NAME
qfw-dir-svc \- manage one QFw directory-service instance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qfw-dir-svc
.I start|run
.B --run-dir
.I path
[
.I options
]
.PP
.B qfw-dir-svc
.I status|stop
.B --run-dir
.I path
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qfw-dir-svc
starts, supervises, inspects, or stops a directory service without owning a
QPM. After a live start it atomically publishes the resolved name and endpoint
to the
.B directory-service.connection-file
configured in
.BR qfw-site.yaml (5).
.PP
In application scope the directory service runs on the head of allocation
group 1. In site scope
.B --node
selects its node. Remote placement requires a Slurm allocation; local mode can
launch only on the calling node.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --run-dir " path"
Private lifecycle state, PID, readiness, and log directory.
.TP
.BI --site-config " path"
Site configuration. The default comes from the activated QFw environment.
.TP
.BI --runtime-config " path"
Runtime configuration used to resolve application allocation policy.
.TP
.BI --scope " application|site"
Select application-owned or site-owned lifetime and placement rules.
.TP
.BI --node " node"
Site-scope launch node. This option is rejected in application scope.
.TP
.BI --timeout " seconds"
Startup timeout.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Resolve and record the plan without starting a process or publishing the
client connection record.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qfw-qpm-svc (1),
.BR qfw-site.yaml (5),
.BR qfw-service-plane (7)
